cially  the agilent bravo  that's a great question and i can feel  this one kristin  so here at mimetis we've actually worked  together  with an academic partner to use the  agilent bravo and successfully loaded  cells both in a gel  as well as against the gel um loading  nearly i would say almost 100 percent  success rate  and so in addition to to loading the  cultures we can actually use the agilent  bravo to  change the culture media add assay  reagents and things like that so it's a  really nice option  to kind of pair all three of the  platforms even  okay thank you we've got another  question here from the audience  um they say it's a really interesting  presentation and have you used biotech  imaging for herpes virus research  yeah so um i'm not sure if we've  directly used it for herpes virus  research but we have  worked with researchers with a variety  of viral infections  and i'd be more than happy to discuss  this kind of one-on-one during the  networking lounge  and go over some of the assays and  applications that you might want to  perform with the biotech imaging  okay and another question here acute  kidney injury is a morbidity  uh associated with covid could we use  these um momentous vessels to also grow  kidney tubules  in fact you can um so similar to the  the way you would have seeded  endothelial tubules you can sort of seed  your  your you know proximal tubule cells into  that perfusion channel and  after they undergo perfusion they'll  form a 3d  proximal tubule-like structure within  the perfusion channel  so definitely feasible okay  another question from the audience what  percentage of non-infected cells  cells and forming leaky tubules  so that's a good question so cells that  are not infected or  not impacted by toxic insult should form  completely leak-type barrier so that's a  hundred percent of the cells that are  not infected  form we type barriers and it's not until  we see that insult do we see any leaking  [Music]  got a couple more questions chris  kristen mentioned that she used both  citation one and the citation five what  is the difference between the two  instruments and is one better than the  other for organoplate imaging  i'll field that one so um the difference  is between the citation one the citation  five so both will work for your imaging  and the organo plate  um they're just slightly different  configurations so the citation one is an  affordable  option for um automated imaging and also  multi-mode microplate detection  there's a limitation in the number of  objectives that it can hold compared to  the citation five so it can hold two  objectives  the citation five can hold six um and  there's some additional limitations in  the  the types of imaging modes so with the  citation 5 you have the full range of  configuration options  and the best way to figure out which one  of those instruments would be great for  you is to just discuss it with a biotech  sales representative and a field  application scientist  and we will help guide you to the right  configuration based on your assays and  applications  okay great thank you so much we've got  another question  in addition to evaluating barrier  integrity is it feasible to measure  tier trans-endothelial electrical  resistance  within the organoplate platform  that's a good question um yes so we've  recently  developed a tier device you can actually  place these um  it's sort of like a plate lid that goes  on top of the lid and there's tiny  electrodes that go into each well  and all at the same time you can measure  the tier value for each of your cultures  and so this is extremely compatible  right now with our epithelial control  cultures and we're working towards  making it compatible with our  endothelial cultures and hope to have  that done by the end of the year  all right we've got one more question we  are hoping to analyze the impact of  infection on endothelial  endothelial cell type junctions how  would you analy